\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary weathers Russia storm, finance troika says. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Roddy \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1998\par 12:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The troika of officials running Hungary's economy said on Friday the country has had to adjust to the Russian crisis but will weather the storm. \par \par \uc2  "I think the most critical part of the process is over," National Bank of Hungary President Gyorgy Suranyi told an economic forum in Budapest, referring to the bank's decision earlier this week to reverse a longterm downward trend and hike key repo interest rates by 100 basis points each. \par \par \uc2  The bank acted to prop up the forint after foreign investors cashed in their holdings on the Budapest bourse, partly to cover losses in Russia, and bond yields rose to some two or three percentage points above the NBH's own issues. \par \par \uc2  But Suranyi, joined by Finance Minister Zsigmond Jarai and Economics Minister Attila Chikan, said the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings had been barely dented, standing now at $8.8 billion, and he had detected an easing on the bonds market. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday in the market we saw even a slight decrease in bonds that mature in more than one year," Suranyi said. \par \par \uc2  Jarai said the impact of the Russian crisis was having an effect on Hungary's economy but he still expected a GDP growth rate of five percent this year. \par \par \uc2  "Russia will influence the Hungarian economy, but we still believe five percent growth is a real expectation for Hungary because of its dependence on the European Union market," Jarai said. \par \par \uc2  "Two months ago we were talking about five to six percent growth, but now we are talking about five percent - so we go down a bit," Jarai told the forum sponsored by Fortune Magazine. \par \par \uc2  He added that the government was determined to maintain budget discipline and would not spend beyond already forecast budget deficits. \par \par \uc2  He also said Hungary's bourse, which dropped more than 40 percent this year, had been hurt because "unfortunately, in the mind of investors Hungary is still part of the Russian empire". \par \par \uc2  "But this is not the case: we are almost part of the European Union and not dependent on Russia," he said. \par \par \uc2  Suranyi said there was no immediate need for any widening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band or any change in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's crawling peg devaluation system, which combats inflation by ratcheting down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a rate of 0.7 percent a month. \par \par \uc2  "There is no pressure at all on the forint, which means that the forint exchange rate system is not only in line with expectations of market players, but is also in line with our sustainability objectives," he said. \par \par \uc2  He added that a cut in U.S. interest rates, which is widely anticipated in global markets following Senate testimony this week by Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan, should be beneficial for Hungary. \par \par \uc2  "If it has any direct impact, then it would rather bring down Hungarian rates or at least would leave them unchanged, but in no way does it mean an upward pressure," Suranyi said. \par \par \uc2  "So I do not think it would have any unfavourable impact, on the contrary I think if it has any effect it would be favourable." \par \par \uc2  Suranyi showed no sympathy for anyone who had speculated against the forint, saying a shortage of forints in the market should be relieved after speculators close out futures positions. \par \par \uc2  "There are no real signs of speculation against the forint ...and those who did are closing out their positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  "...This temporary liquidity shortage is due to the fact that speculators have to close their futures positions, but we do not think the National Bank should help speculators reduce their losses," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I do not think that it would make sense for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pump extra liquidity into the system, even temporarily, although there are several tools in the hands of the National Bank for such a step." \par \par \uc2  ((Michael Roddy, Budapest newsroom ++36 1 327 4040 \par \par \uc2  budapest.newsroom@reuter.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9p01xvx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
