\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary NBH says economy weathering Russian crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Roddy \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1998\par 17:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Sept 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gyorgy Suranyi said on Tuesday that Hungary's economy was weathering the Russian storm without the need for any major adjustments. \par \par \uc2  Suranyi, in a news conference and separate interview with Reuters Television, said no major actions were needed to protect the forint from outflows of investor capital and said Hungary might beat year-end inflation targets, though next year's GDP performance could suffer if global trends continue. \par \par \uc2  Analysts mostly applauded his statements as helping to dispel some negative speculation about the state of the Hungarian economy in the midst of global market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "It's not so much news as a psychological announcement," said Norbert Toth, analyst at ING Barings in Budapest. \par \par \uc2  Suranyi quashed recent market speculation that the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) might widen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, which allows the forint to float in a 4.5 percent range against a basket of dollars and marks. \par \par \uc2  "I rule out the possibility of widening of the band because for the time being it might create depreciation in a wider band and any depreciation is not reasonable for the time being," Suranyi said. \par \par \uc2  He said a "nominal" hike in interest rates was a more likely option to prop up the forint, hit by a wave of foreign investors liquidating their Hungarian share portfolios. \par \par \uc2  He acknowledged there was pressure on the forint but denied that the amount withdrawn had caused a significant dent in the NBH's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $9 billion. \par \par \uc2  "We would speak about a couple of tens of millions of dollars (spent on active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," he said. \par \par \uc2  Suranyi said the Hungarian economy remained on target for reducing inflation, one of Hungary's persistent problems. He said the bank might be able to lower its year-end CPI target to 13 percent year on year for December, from the current forecast of between 13 and 14 percent. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot exclude a downward modification of the inflation target for the current year," he said. \par \par \uc2  He also lent his support to the government's moves to cut the crawling peg \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluator, which ratchets down the value of the forint on a monthly basis. \par \par \uc2  The government has announced that the peg will be reduced to 0.7 percent from 0.8 percent on October 1 and has mooted a further cut to 0.6 percent is likely for January. \par \par \uc2  "We have a good chance for a (further) cut in the crawl from the beginning of next year," Suranyi said, noting the prerequisite for doing so was "responsible, tight fiscal policy". \par \par \uc2  Suranyi said Hungary might have to cut its 1999 Gross Domestic Product (GDP) growth target by one percentage point to 4.5 percent if negative global trends emanating from Russia, Asia and Latin American continue. \par \par \uc2  But he said that it was time for the investment community to stop lumping Poland, Hungary and Czech Republic, all three of which will enter NATO this year and are expected to join the EU early next century, with other emerging market countries. \par \par \uc2  "I would like to challenge this categorisation," he said, adding that the economic performance and the institutional and legal structures of the three countries were "entirely different from so-called emerging markets". \par \par \uc2  Istvan Racz, analyst for Credit Suisse/First Boston, said that the NBH seemed to be handling the capital outflows without too much difficult and agreed with Suranyi that Hungary was only moderately affected by the Russian crisis. \par \par \uc2  "What he said on the general state of the economy I would fully agree to," Racz said. \par \par \uc2  ((Michael Roddy, Budapest newsroom ++ 36 1 327 4040 budapest.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9f016go\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
