\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS-Hungary, Poland swim vs  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  tide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gill Tudor \par \uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par 18:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Two central European countries are swimming against the tide of devaluation, rumour and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  collapse sweeping the emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say by cutting their crawling devaluation rates, the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation which compensates for domestic inflation, Poland and Hungary are keeping a firm eye on their own economic fundamentals - especially slowing inflation. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at these countries in terms of fundamentals, they are several leagues higher than Russia and most of the Asian countries," said Nigel Rendell, emerging markets strategist at Santander Investment Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  But such confident gestures could backfire if they are aimed purely at countering market bearishness, the analysts added. \par \par \uc2  "Trying to outwit the market is a dangerous game to play for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or finance ministry," Rendell said. \par \par \uc2  Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orban said this week the decision to ease the pace of the forint's monthly crawling devaluation to 0.7 percent from 0.8 percent from October 1 was aimed partly at fending off adverse market speculation. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said emerging market sentiment was currently so jittery that any mention of devaluation, even if accompanied by concrete moves to the contrary, risked attracting exactly the kind of speculative attention it sought to avoid. \par \par \uc2  "As a statement, it's not the one they should have made," said Andrew Kenningham, an emerging markets analyst at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  The announcement lent little support to the forint, which continues to languish at the weakest end of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4.5 percent wide \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. On Thursday morning it stood 2.22 percent below its target central parity. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be forced to widen the trading band, although it was unlikely to go back on its decision to slow the devaluation rate of the target parity because of the negative signal that would give. \par \par \uc2  News on Wednesday that Poland's Monetary Policy Council (RPP) was slowing the zloty devaluation rate to 0.5 percent a month from 0.65 percent, and cutting a key interest rate by one percentage point, also had a defiant ring. \par \par \uc2  "It is high time that foreign capital adjusts to what is happening in Poland," RPP member Boguslav Grabowski told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The Polish moves, the latest in a series of steps in recent months to slow the zloty's downward crawl and cut rates, have won broad market approval and followed indications that consumer prices fell for a second consecutive month in August. \par \par \uc2  The zloty surged nearly one percentage point on Thursday to 4.71 percent above its parity, set daily by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of five Western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Poland was particularly insulated from the risk of sudden outflows of investment capital. \par \par \uc2  Only about $4 billion of foreign "hot money" was left in Poland - $1.5 billion in the Treasuries market and $2.5 billion in equities - against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $27 billion, Kenningham said. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, hot money equalled 60 percent of reserves in Hungary. But Hungary's balance of payments was relatively strong, with a current-account deficit only half the size of foreign direct investment in the country, Kenningham added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Poland and Hungary are at or near the top of the emerging markets pile where fundamentals are concerned. \par \par \uc2  Both countries have seen big falls in inflation in the past few years, and have sensible and stable fiscal policies. Both also have relatively limited formal trade exposure to Russia. \par \par \uc2  Tim Ash, an economist at West Merchant bank in London, said Poland's current account remained vulnerable to Russian fallout because of previously high levels of informal "suitcase trade" - Russians buying cheap Polish goods for resale at home. \par \par \uc2  "There was definitely scope for interest rate cuts, but the loss of the suitcase trade makes cutting the crawl rather strange," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sluggish second-quarter growth in Germany, a more important trading partner for both countries, could also weigh on the Polish and Hungarian economies, Ash added. Germany's annual gross domestic product grew in the quarter by just 1.7 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said another major risk remained - the possibility that devaluations elsewhere could spark a further round of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-offs in which rational fundamentals took a back seat. \par \par \uc2  "If we see another swipe at emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  I think all bets for Central Europe would probably be off," Rendell said. ((London Newsroom +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00tgx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
