\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary minister says economy sound amid chaos. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Roddy \par \uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 18:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Hungarian Minister of Economy Attila Chikan said on Thursday that the economy remained sound amid global turmoil and urged investors to distinguish among emerging market countries. \par \par \uc2  "Investors still tend to lump us together too much," Chikan said at a news conference. "They must learn to differentiate." \par \par \uc2  Chikan said that despite some pressure on the forint, which has been hit by investors withdrawing money from the stock market to cover losses elsewhere, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ample reserves. \par \par \uc2  "The National Bank is sticking on its exchange policy and they are ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary," Chikan said. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  has ample foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," he added, referring to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves estimated at some $9 billion. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on two days last week as the Hungarian bourse slipped to near 17-month lows, but traders have said the bank's total dollar outlay was not more than about $100 million. \par \par \uc2  Despite the forint being buffeted by the Russian crisis, Hungary plans to go ahead with further cuts in the crawling peg devaluation system that reduces the value of the forint on a monthly basis, Chikan said. \par \par \uc2  "We would like to continue the decrease of the devalution rate of the forint," Chikan said. "Our plan is, if nothing dramatic happens, then the first of January will see another 0.1 percent decrease." \par \par \uc2  Hungary introduced a monthly crawling peg devaluator in 1995 designed to ratchet down then-rampant inflation. The devaluator stands at 0.8 percent but the government has already announced that it will be cut to 0.7 percent on October 1. \par \par \uc2  Chikan said he saw no immediate reason to revise downwards government Gross Domestic Product growth projections of five to 5.5 percent in 1999. \par \par \uc2  The government's forecast had been based on the assumption of three percent growth in Western Europe, he said. \par \par \uc2  Only if the growth projections for Western Europe, which consumes almost 80 percent of Hungarian exports, changed substantially would Hungary have to revise its forecasts, he said. \par \par \uc2  "However, I have to say that when we talked privately...five percent was the lower limit of the growth projection," Chikan said. "Maybe it won't be higher, but we hope that it will not be lower." \par \par \uc2  Earlier, Credit Suisse First Boston said it expected Hungarian GDP growth may be slightly below the government target of at least five percent in 1999 due to the Russian crisis. \par \par \uc2  "The growth target is probably too optimistic - growth is more likely to be 4-4.5 percent in view of the Russian crisis," CSFB said in a weekly update on European emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  ((Michael Roddy, Budapest newsroom ++ 36 1 327 4040 \par \par \uc2  budapest.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93023we\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
