\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary move to boost confidence in forint. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1998\par 12:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Aug 31 (Reuters) - Hungary's decision to slow the devaluation of its forint amid capital outflows caused by the Russian crisis was unexpected but supported by economic fundamentals, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet decided on Saturday to cut to 0.7 percent from 0.8 percent the forint's monthly devaluation rate - a regime in place since 1995 to curb runaway inflation at that time. The change will take effect on October 1. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the measure could boost confidence in the forint, which has taken a beating in the Russian crisis, prompting the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for an unprecedented three times in one day last week. \par \par \uc2  "I had not expected this measure, but it has not surprised me and I think it is a very good move," said Attila Torok of Postabank Securities. \par \par \uc2  The forint depreciated more than two percent in the past few weeks as foreign investors sold Hungarian shares and bonds and withdrew the proceeds to cover losses suffered in Russia. \par \par \uc2  The forint approached the weakest point of the 4.5-percent-wide \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band several times last week but was turned back by NBH \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the forint opened stronger against the NBH \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, at 1.5 percent below parity, up from Friday's close of 1.7 percent below midline. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the NBH \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were successful, although they had a mostly psychological impact because the total amount spent was estimated at only $100 million. \par \par \uc2  They said the joint decision by the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the depreciation rate would help to undercut widespread speculation of higher interest rates or a widening of the forint's flotation band. \par \par \uc2  The latter measures could decrease pressure on the forint temporarily, but would seriously undermine confidence in the exchange rate regime and monetary policy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It would have been a serious mistake to widen the flotation band or devalue the forint as it would have seemed to justify speculation (against the forint)," Torok said. \par \par \uc2  Hungary introduced its crawling peg devaluation regime in March 1995, reducing speculation against the forint and keeping it at the strong end of of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band for most of the past three years. \par \par \uc2  The monthly devalution rate has been reduced incrementally to 0.8 percent from the original 1.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  The decision on a further cut in the rate would strengthen confidence in the forint, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "On the one hand, this measure increases real interest rate yields (on government securities) to foreigners," said Gergely Forian Szabo of K&H Brokerhaz. \par \par \uc2  "On the other hand, it strengthens confidence." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the move was justified by strong economic fundamentals and a high level of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which amount to $9 billion. \par \par \uc2  "The move will obviously have a reassuring impact on investors and make them think whether it is worth mixing Hungary with Russia," Torok said. \par \par \uc2  Torok estimated that foreigners held Hungarian bonds worth $1.5 billion and shares worth about $2 billion, with the total figure remaining well below foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  In the longer term, the forint was expected to regain some ground, but analysts said it was unlikely to return to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resistance level at the strong end of the band. \par \par \uc2  ((Budapest newsroom +36 1 327 4044 budapest.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8v01vjr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
