\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hungary \highlight\uc2 /Devaluation Rate -2: Follows Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 1998\par 12:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut the monthly devaluation rate of the forint to 0.7% from the present rate of 0.8% effective Oct. 1, Prime Minister Viktor Orban announced Saturday, state news agency MTI reported. \par \par \uc2  Orban made the announcement after a meeting with top economic officials in the government including Minister of Finance Zsigmond Jarai, Minister of Economic Affairs Attila Chikan, and National Bank of Hungary President Gyorgy Suranyi. \par \par \uc2  Suranyi said after the meeting that the step would "have a beneficial effect" on inflationary expectations in Hungary, state radio reported. \par \par \uc2  The central banker repeated his previous statements that there won't be any extraordinary devaluations of the forint, which weakened sharply during the past week as investors pulled their money out of Hungary on fears of contagion from Russia's economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on two days during the past week to stabilize the forint, its first \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1995. \par \par \uc2  Suranyi also said there are no plans to widen the forint's trading band. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to move a maximum of 2.25% from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's parity rate, which is calculated using a fixing basket of 30% dollars and 70% Deutsche marks. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 29-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1026GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8t07ehb\pa