\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP/Emerging Mkts Fall -2: Russia Rebound Unconvincing \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 18:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Russian stocks rebounded slightly Friday from dramatic falls earlier in the week, but traders cautioned that the move was exaggerated by the fact that volume has dried up to a trickle. \par \par \uc2  The Russian Trading System index closed up 5.6% at 66.77 points from Thursday's 63.2 points. But overall volume was less than $2.2 million, and market participants said the overall mood remained grim. \par \par \uc2  The ruble (RUR) also rebounded slightly, and traders said interbank quotes for the dollar ranged from RUR10 to RUR13, compared with quotes of RUR12 to RUR14 on Thursday. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later announced its official rate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was RUR7.905 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The bounce in Russia didn't spill over to most other emerging markets. Polish shares plummeted to their lowest level for more than two years as foreign investors continued to flee the market. Most stocks fell by the daily allowable maximum of 10%. Market participants said the market has room to fall further. \par \par \uc2  "We haven't reached the lowest levels yet," said Andreas Roithner, salesman at Raiffeisen Capital & Investment Polska. "There are no support levels anymore, there is free fall. At the moment, everybody is trying to get out." \par \par \uc2  The zloty (PLN) closed sharply lower, with the dollar fixed at PLN3.8150, up from Thursday's fixing of PLN3.7950. However, volumes on both the share and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were low. \par \par \uc2  That was in contrast to Hungary, where volume on the stock market remained heavy at 21 billion forints (HUF) as the Budapest Stock Exchange's BUX index closed at 5167.71, 5.5% down on the day. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  twice in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market to stabilize the forint (HUF). At 1000 GMT, the close of trade,\par the dollar was at HUF226.47-HUF226.54, down from HUF226.75-HUF226.85\par prior to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and sharply lower from HUF228.30-HUF228.50 at\par Thursday's close.\par -  -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 28-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1640GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1192 4207 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8s07dx8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
