\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP/Emerging Markets/Russia -3: Forint Steady, Zloty Up \b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 14:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Israeli companies have responded to the events in Russia and Asia by repaying their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowings, contributing to the continued slide of the shekel (ILS) in brisk trading. \par \par \uc2  Around 0910 GMT, the shekel was trading around ILS3.7840 to the dollar, down 1.3% from ILS3.7360 at the Bank of Israel fixing Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Companies are paying back foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans because they feel "there's too much risk in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said John Borsteinas, dealing room manager at Koor Capital Markets Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Declines on the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange have contributed to that perception of increased risk, Borsteinas said. \par \par \uc2  But despite the sharp drop in Hungarian share prices, the forint (HUF) was steady at midmorning, with no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 1995. Around 0825 GMT, the dollar was at HUF226.60-226.90, up from HUF226.30-226.70 at Wednesday's close. The mark was at HUF125.53-125.58, down from HUF126.10-126.15. \par \par \uc2  The Polish zloty (PLN) was stronger against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket early Thursday on speculative domestic buying, although Russia's economic turmoil continues to keep foreign investors out the market. \par \par \uc2  Around 0825 GMT, the dollar was at PLN3.7400, down from about PLN3.8000 at the opening and down from Wednesday's fixing at PLN3.7790. The Deutsche mark was at PLN2.0720, down from Wednesday's fixing at PLN2.0880. \par \par \uc2  The Czech koruna (CZK) was also higher in early trading. At 0720 GMT, traders were quoting the dollar at CZK33.513 to CZK33.560, down from CZK33.630 to CZK33.640 at the end of dealing Wednesday and slightly down from CZK33.587 to CZK33.633 at 1000 GMT Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, falling oil prices led to a decline in the Saudi riyal (SAR). At about 1115 GMT, the dollar was trading at SAR3.7680-SAR3.7750. This compares with SAR3.7535-SAR3.7545 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Paul Hannon 44-171-832 9513 phannon@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 27-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1256GMT \par \par \uc2  1195 4209 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8r07ap4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
