\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Hungarian c.bank acts, East  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  lurch. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Crosby \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 16:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the forint as eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  battled a wave of volatility and investor nerves triggered by uncertainty over Russia's debt restructuring deal. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to reduce forint fluctuations that it said were unjustified by domestic economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "The main reason for the current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to soothe the exaggerated exchange rate movements unjustified by macroeconomic indicators," NBH managing director Adam Farkas told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came when the forint traded at around 1.5 to 1.6 percent below parity, a safe distance from the weaker end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. At the time of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the forint traded at between 226.30 and 226.50 to the dollar, and the move pushed it up to around 226.00. \par \par \uc2  The forint closed at 226.30/80, compared to Tuesday's close at 225.20/40, while against the basket it finished at around 1.40 percent below parity as compared to Tuesday's close at 1.15 percent under the midrange. \par \par \uc2  Ukraine's hryvnia was also hit hard closing at 2.2491 per dollar on the republic's Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Exchange, down from 2.2485 on Tuesday and just under its trade band ceiling. \par \par \uc2  The Polish zloty and the Czech and Slovak crowns also lost ground throughout the day, but traders said that while market sentiment overall was bearish, the central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should weather the storm in the end on strong fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "The debt deal is fairly bad, but this was expected. Confirmation the deal is going through caused a slight correction in eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but now markets such as Poland and the Czech Republic should concentrate on their own fundamentals," said Rob Taylor, a trader at Nomura. \par \par \uc2  "I can see further weakening ahead, but I can't see any more contagion unless we have a huge devaluation of the rouble, which is likely to be the fear at the moment due to MICEX trading being suspended today," he added. \par \par \uc2  Late on Tuesday Russia announced details of a plan to restructure $40 billion of short-term domestic debt into longer-term paper. The plan aims to cut the cost of servicing the state's domestic debt. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the plan was an effective default and left unanswered many crucial questions that could hit investor confidence in Russia for years to come. \par \par \uc2  "As far as the weakening (in eastern Europe) today, that has been on the back of the default in Russia and just a confirmation of it, we shouldn't expect things to spiral out of control from here unless things get worse on the devaluation side on Russia," said another London-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. \par \par \uc2  The rouble slumped against the German mark on the main \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  voided dollar trades and said it would not set a dollar rate for the day. It was fixed at 7.6 marks per dollar versus 4.4995 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed at 0.94/0.23 percent, stronger than parity, against Tuesday's 2.83/2.56 and the opening 0.40/-0.40 percent. The Czech crown also eased to around 18.64 against the mark from an opening around 18.345. \par \par \uc2  "The crown is following the zloty and rouble lower, but on a much smaller scale. There are still questions over the Russia deal but it doesn't effect the Czech crown that directly," said Breta Tichanek, a trader at ING Barings . \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown eased slightly against its mark/dollar basket but dealers said the move came on local selling rather than on negative sentiment from Russia. \par \par \uc2  However, the continued uncertainty sparked by the Russian financial crisis once again affected the Slovak money market where interest rates shot up in nervous trade. One-day interbank rates rose to 26/27 percent, bid/offer, on Wednesday from Tuesday's 16/16.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01kzu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
