\par\par \b \uc2  Central European  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Face Weakness On Russia News \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christopher Wellisz \par \uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 18:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WARSAW (Dow Jones)--Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were further buffeted on Wednesday by ill economic winds blowing from Russia, and analysts said more volatility can be expected. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since early 1995 to sell dollars in support of the flagging forint. Poland's zloty and the Czech koruna both weakened in response to the plunging Russian ruble. \par \par \uc2  Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain acutely sensitive to movements of the ruble, analysts said, but they added that sound economies and limited trade and financial links with Russia should shield them from severe damage. \par \par \uc2  "We are looking at continued weakness and volatility" said Anthony Thomas, an economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson. "But to turn central Europe into a full blown crisis we would have to see not just Russia but China and Latin America go as well." \par \par \uc2  All three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reacted quickly to news of Russia's plan to restructure ruble-denominated debt, which analysts worried could result in huge losses for foreign investors. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took another hit later in the day amid news of the ruble's 69% plunge against the Deutsche mark. \par \par \uc2  Under the deal, announced late Tuesday, holders of Russian treasury debt maturing before the end of 1999 must trade in their securities for a variety of new ones that mature much later and yield less. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars when the forint dropped to 1.7% weaker than the midpoint of its trading band, down from 1.18% at the close of trading Tuesday. The forint is allowed to fluctuate by a maximum of 2.25% from the midpoint. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 26-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1617GMT \par \par \uc2  1193 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8q07aqd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
