\par\par \b \uc2  Europe Focus -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Were Ripe For Profit-Taking \b0\par\par\uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1998\par 15:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The Czech koruna floats freely, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally to reduce volatility. \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Poland have managed exchange-rate regimes, a factor that makes them less vulnerable to outside shocks. \par \par \uc2  The Polish zloty is fixed to a basket of five Western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is devalued at a rate of 0.65% per month. It is allowed to fluctuate within plus or minus 10% of the central parity rate. \par \par \uc2  Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the forint to move a maximum 2.25% from the midpoint, which is fixed using a basket of 30% dollar and 70% Deutsche mark. The bank devalues the forint by 0.8% each month against the basket. \par \par \uc2  Russia and Asia aside, local factors were also at work on Tuesday. The soaring zloty and koruna were vulnerable to profit-taking, analysts said. And the Polish and Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also weakened by expectations of interest rate cuts in both countries. \par \par \uc2  To varying degrees in all three countries, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have strengthened as investors buy Treasury bills and bonds in search of higher yields than are available in Western Europe. \par \par \uc2  These investors see central Europe as a "convergence play." As Poland, Hungary and the Czech Republic move toward European Union membership in a few years, economic policies will become more restrictive and interest rates will decline to Western European levels. \par \par \uc2  "We have seen something of a euphoric period for central European markets over the past several weeks," said David Simmonds, an economist at Citibank in London. "These investors are looking at these markets as the new Spain or Greece," he said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1354GMT \par \par \uc2  1089 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8b06rgp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
