\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary forint seen steady at current level. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 1997\par 19:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Dec 4 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint is likely to remain at current levels in the next few months after falling on recent turmoil on emerging markets, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "I think the forint is weaker due to the turmoil which began in Asia and overspilled to the U.S. and Europe," said Gabor Oblath of economic think-tank Kopint-Datorg. \par \par \uc2  "Quite a few foreign investors withdrew money from eastern Europe and the consequences spread to Hungary, although it was quite unjustified by the state of the economy." \par \par \uc2  The forint fell to its lowest point in two years on October 28 to 194.50 against the dollar, in reaction to Asia's economic problems. This represented a level of 1.24 percent above parity, the mid-point of the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4.5 per cent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  range for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since then the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen to two percent above parity, closing on Thursday at 200.40 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Before its late-October fall, the forint was usually trading at the National Bank's resistance level, or 2.25 percent above parity, against a basket comprising 30 percent dollars and 70 percent marks. \par \par \uc2  The forint's record fall came on the same day as the Budapest Stock Exchange, depressed by a previous collapse on the Hong Kong bourse, suffered its largest ever daily drop. The BUX index dived 1,294.54 points or 16.5 percent to 6,540.74 points. \par \par \uc2  Since then, the BUX has climbed to a closing 7,126.56 points on Thursday but has remained volatile at levels well below its record 8,627.95 points reached on August 7. \par \par \uc2  Peter Bihari of Budapest Bank said foreign investors are estimated to have withdrawn about 50 to 60 billion forints from Hungary, fearing an overspill affect of financial problems in Asian countries, including South Korea and Japan. \par \par \uc2  "There is an uncertainty factor on international markets now and this has an unfavourable impact on emerging markets in general," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors burned themselves and now they are licking their wounds," he added. "The forint's movement is likely to remain hectic in the next few months and it won't stick to the top of the (4.5 percent wide) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  range." \par \par \uc2  However, analysts agreed that the state of the Hungarian economy did not justify the weakening of the forint. \par \par \uc2  "The current account is so good and other economic indicators are improving in such a way as to make a weak forint unjustified," Oblath said. "This is simply a psychosis." \par \par \uc2  However, most analysts did not regard a weaker forint as a threat to the economy and some of them even said it was a positive factor as it scared away hot money from the country. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it would be bad if the forint remains unchanged and short-term capital inflow is at a low level," Oblath said, adding that the National Bank of Hungary's losses offsetting the inflationary impact of direct investment and other capital inflow have been high this year. \par \par \uc2  While a forint trading below the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level makes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more risky for foreign speculators, analysts said the forint was unlikely to ease substantially further unless the tension on world markets increases. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think a substantial weakening is likely," Oblath said. \par \par \uc2  He added that the forint's strength will also depend on the monetary policy the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows from January 1, when the monthly crawling peg devaluator will be cut to 0.9 percent from one percent. \par \par \uc2  "If it offers a high real yield compared with inflation and the devaluation rate is reduced, the forint may move back towards the National Bank's resistance level," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Budapest newsroom +36 1 327 4044 budapest.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc401kwy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
