\par\par \b \uc2  Asia-Induced Volatility Seen Persisting In Hungary's Markets \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jack Grone \par \uc2  Staff Reporter \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 1997\par 16:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST -(Dow Jones)- If Hungary's equities market was an airliner, the "fasten seat belts" sign would be blinking, and analysts who have watched the market slide 17% in the last month say turbulence in global financial markets is here to stay for at least a few weeks. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a "guilt-by-association" perception of emerging-market economies among investors means that events in Asia are likely to have deeper and longer-lasting repercussions in eastern European markets such as Hungary than in nations to the west. \par \par \uc2  The mood grew more uncertain this week as it became increasingly evident that South Korea is the latest victim of the financial tumult gripping a number of Asian nations. That means investors holding Hungarian assets who have positioned themselves defensively probably will sit tight, perhaps until year end, onlookers say. "The word is 'caution'," said an emerging-markets sales executive at a European investment bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "We are trying to find something to follow," said Gabriella Bor, equities analyst at New York Brokerhaz in Budapest. "For the next few weeks, the volatility should continue, but then I think the market will stabilize." \par \par \uc2  "The short-term developments are still hard to predict. People (in Hungary) will have both eyes on Asia," said Spencer Jakab, head of Hungarian equities research at Credit Suisse First Boston in London. "Korea is the real litmus test. If that stays together, I think things will be okay." \par \par \uc2  Jakab said that based on its cash flow, shares of oil-and-gas company MOL Rt. would be fairly valued around 5,600 forints ($28.55), 33% above Thursday's closing price of 4,210 forints. Another of Jakab's picks is tile maker Zalakeramia Rt. Based on its earnings and capacity growth, the stock should trade at around 12,000 forints, up 39% from 8,650 forints at Thursday's close, according to the analyst. \par \par \uc2  Also supporting the market is the resilient forint, which the National Bank of Hungary devalues 1.0% monthly against a basket composed 30% of dollars and 70% of German marks. The forint weakened moderately after equities began to slide in late October, but has since stabilized well on the strong side of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. \par \par \uc2  The devaluations, along with \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fast-growing exports and strong investment inflows, mean the forint is among the least risky of Eastern Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a recent report by brokerage firm Merrill Lynch. On the negative side, buying interest may be depressed over the rest of 1997 by the defensive, cash-heavy positions in which emerging-market investment funds now find themselves, particularly open-ended funds that could face large redemptions by investors. \par \par \uc2  Also unclear is the extent to which domestic investors' local holdings are still leveraged with "Lombard loans" from Budapest securities firms. Such loans, collateralized with securities, allowed domestic investors to leverage their way into ever-riskier positions earlier this year, when the BUX peaked at nearly 8500 points. But when the market started to fall, securities firms seeking to minimize their losses sold the shares collateralizing the loans, pushing the market further downward. \par \par \uc2  A broker at a Budapest securities firm cited isolated stories of investors forced to sell possessions such as cars to meet cash calls. But the deleveraging makes the market healthier, he added. More evidence that investors are pulling in their horns came this week, when the Budapest Stock Exchange said it had suspended the per-investor limit on BUX futures because the number of net open positions had fallen below 50,000. The limits, designed to protect against surges of short-term funds flooding the futures market, took effect in September. \par \par \uc2  And with Asia's problems "not over by a long shot," Eastern Europe's worries remain, Jakab said. "Unfortunately, there's not only more turbulence ahead, but more bad times as well," he added. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1192 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtbk012w7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
